-- title:
--   Igusa good reduction for the two-chart ℤ₍ₚ₎-model of X(Γ)
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $M \geq 1$ and a subgroup $\Gamma \leq \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ with $\Gamma_1(M) \leq \Gamma \leq \Gamma_0(M)$, and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid M$. Let $F = \mathtt{qExpFunctionFieldC}\ \mathbb{Q}\ \Gamma$ be the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set of quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}(p_f)/\mathrm{intSeriesC}(p_g)$, where $f, g$ are modular forms of a common weight $k$ on $\Gamma$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[\![q]\!]$ and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}(p_g) \neq 0$. Let $j \in F$ be nonzero and assume its underlying Laurent series is `jqModC`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image of $\mathtt{jNum} = E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$-type power series $\mathrm{eisenstein4}^3 \cdot \mathrm{dedekindEtaUnitInv}$; thus $j$ has the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant. Let $R = \mathtt{ratLocalizedAt}\ p \subset \mathbb{Q}$ be the subring of rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$, i.e. $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$. Consider the two-chart model $\mathtt{TwoChartIntegralModel}\ R\ F\ j$, the pushout of the two maps $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the inclusions of the chart algebras $\mathtt{chartAlgFin}\ R\ F\ j = \mathtt{chartAlg}\ R\ F\ \{j\}$ and $\mathtt{chartAlgInf}\ R\ F\ j = \mathtt{chartAlg}\ R\ F\ \{j^{-1}\}$ into the middle chart, with its structure morphism $c$ to $\mathrm{Spec}\, R$ induced by the $R$-algebra structures. The assertion is the conjunction: $c$ is proper, $c$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$, and $c$ is geometrically integral.
--
--   This is Igusa's theorem on good reduction away from the level, in the form asserting that the normalisation of the $j$-line in the function field of $X(\Gamma)$ over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is a proper smooth curve with geometrically integral fibres. It underlies the construction of the integral models of modular curves at primes away from the level used in the study of their Jacobians and special fibres, and is cited by the corresponding statements for full level and for the models $X_H$ at $p$.
